<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01871</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a860ef95-560f-4bfa-a75a-245dabb2c12d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">Here is a diagram of a rectangle with side lengths \(x+1\) and \(x+3\). Use this diagram to show that \((x+1)(x+3)\) and \(x^2+4x+3\) are equivalent expressions.</para>
            <para id="para-00002">
                      <media alt="Large rectangle, divided into 4 smaller rectangles. Left rectangle on top labeled x, Right rectangle on top labeled 3. Top rectangle on left side labeled x, bottom rectangle on left side labeled 1.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">
                      <media alt="Area model multiplying x+3 and x+1">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00006">\(x \cdot x =x^2\)
    \(1 \cdot x=x\)
    \(x \cdot 3=3x\)
    \(1 \cdot 3=3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Adding these together gives \(x^2+4x+3\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Draw diagrams to help you write an equivalent expression for each of the following:</para>
            <para id="para-00009">\((x+5)^2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00012">The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(x^2+10x+25\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">\(2x(x+4)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(2x^2+8x\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00017">\((2x+1)(x+3)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00018">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00020">The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(2x^2+7x+3\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00021">\((x+m)(x+n)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00022">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00024">The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(x^2+xn+mx+xn\) or \(x^2+(m+n)x+mn\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00025">Write an equivalent expression for each expression without drawing a diagram. Use the distributive property to help. Remember FOIL for multiplying binomials: Multiply the First terms, the Outer terms, the Inner terms, and the Last terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00026">\((x+2)(x+6)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00029">\(x^2+8x+12\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00030">\((x+5)(2x+10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00033">\(2x^2+20x+50\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</title>
      <para id="para-00034">Watch the following video to learn more about using diagrams to find equivalent quadratic expressions.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d74c6bb24c118c9ee654b330391716aa3d0144f"/>
      </media></section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00004">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00035">Image 1</para>
          <para id="para-00036">
                  <media alt="Square, side length = x. 5 rectangles, width = x, length = 1. 6 squares with side length = 1.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_03.svg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00037">Image 2</para>
          <para id="para-00038">
                  <media alt="Rectangle decomposed into smaller squares and rectangles">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_04.svg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00039">Are Image 1 and Image 2 equivalent? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00041">Yes, because \(x \cdot x+5(1x)+6(1)=(x+2)(x+3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00042">What does this tell you about an equivalent expression for \(x^2+5x+6\)?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00044">This shows that an equivalent expression is \((x+2)(x+3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00045">Is there a different non-zero number of 1-by-1 squares that we could use instead that would allow us to arrange the combined figures into a single large rectangle?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00047">We could use four 1-by-1 squares. This would give the expression \(x^2+5x+4\). The rectangle would be \((x + 4)(x + 1)\).
      
      (<media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_05.jpg"/></media>)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
  </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00048">Which expression is equivalent to \((x +2) (x + 4)\)?</para>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Multiplying Binomials</title>
      <para id="para-00049">Write an expression equivalent to \((x + 2)(x + 5)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute. Use FOIL. Multiply the First terms, Outer terms, Inner terms, and Last terms.</para>
      <para id="para-00051">
          <media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING X PLUS 2 TIMES X PLUS 5. ON THE TOP, IN RED, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO 5. ON THE BOTTOM, IN BLUE, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 2 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 2 TO 5.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Write out the four terms.
  \(x^2+5x+2x+10\)</para>
      <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
  \(x^2+7x+10\)</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Multiplying Binomials</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00054">Write an expression equivalent to \((x + 7)(x + 3)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00056">Here is how to multiply binomial expressions:</para>
            <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute.</para>
            <para id="para-00058">
                      <media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING X PLUS 7 TIMES X PLUS 3. ON THE TOP, IN RED, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO 3. ON THE BOTTOM, IN BLUE, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 7 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 7 TO 3.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_08_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Write out the four terms.
    \(x^2+3x+7x+10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
    \(x^2+10x+21\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
